          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane explicitly presents her current loving state as true. The qualification “as a sister” specifies that the love is sisterly rather than romantic, without weakening its force as an avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows sisterly affection but does not itself undertake marriage, travel, service, or any other obligation. “As a sister” characterizes the kind of love rather than committing Jane to a defined sisterly role. The surrounding turn expressly rejects an asserted obligation, so the separate discussion of accompanying St John should not be transferred onto the target.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The words are integrated into a deliberate explanatory sentence giving Jane's reason for having been willing to venture much. Nothing in the supplied representation makes the focal clause a reflex-like affective discharge rather than an assertion, despite the emotionally strained conversation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression's core force is adequately captured as a truth-presenting avowal of sisterly love. Its contextual role in explaining Jane's prior willingness is a discourse function, not a distinct core force requiring an O score.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is answering St John's claim that she would dishonourably break a promise concerning India. She denies being obligated, explains that her willingness to venture much with him arose from admiration, trust, and sisterly love, and maintains that she will not undertake the proposed course because she expects the climate would kill her.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal “I love you” contains no undertaking; it avows sisterly affection. Jane's surrounding statements concern a separate, disputed proposal to accompany St John and expressly deny an obligation to go to India. St John replies, “Ah! you are afraid of yourself,” which neither accepts nor reciprocates the avowal, so there is no demonstrated uptake of it as a commitment.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognise the passage as being from Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the represented avowal and supplies some narrator-level evidence of Jane's distress and concern, but it cannot conclusively establish every aspect of sincerity or relationship history. St John's immediate reply does not clarify how he interprets the sisterly-love clause specifically.\"}"
        }
